   "text": {
    "L1": "To decide whether something moves, compare its position with a reference point over time.",
    "L2": "A passenger can be at rest compared with a train seat, but moving compared with the platform.",
    "L3": "This is why the belief that motion needs no reference point is not true.",
    "L4": "Linear motion follows a straight line, circular motion follows a circle, and oscillatory motion goes to and fro about a middle position.",
    "L5": "A train on a straight track is linear, a fan tip is circular, and a swing is oscillatory.",
    "L6": "One object can show two motions at once: a rolling bicycle wheel turns round while the bicycle moves straight ahead.",
    "L7": "Now check the idea: what motion does a child on a swing show?"
   },
